{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Module","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Abel","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic"],"declarations":38,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.markov_stabilisation_fails_on_repeat","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.markov_stabilisation_fails_on_repeat","kind":"theorem","line":773,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L773-L804","doc":"**The once-only-occurrence condition is NECESSARY, for every `q`.**\n\nCombining the two: if the interface generator already occurs in the word, Markov\nstabilisation would force `z² = h·z + 1`, and convention D's `z` misses that by a\nstrictly negative amount at every `q > 0`. So `interface_scalar`'s hypothesis\n**cannot** be assumed on a repeated generator — the collapse of the interface to\nthe single scalar `μ = a·z + b` is available only when the generator occurs once.\n\nThis is a scope-(b) structural exclusion and the invariant is cited: the defect's\nclosed form `-(q²-q+1)(q²+q+1)/(q²+1)²`, whose factors are positive-definite. It\nis not a numerical near-miss at one pin.\n\nMeasured counterpart: `computations/probes/colour_weighting_markov_universality_probe.py`\n(PR #6319) fits `k` in `tr_M ∝ (d + k)` from the traces alone and finds `k = z_D` to\n`1e-48` on four canonical nuclear words — each of which uses its interface generator\nexactly once — and finds the identity **breaks** (`k - z_D = 1.2285`) on the same\ndeuteron word with one extra `σ` on that generator. The Lean says why the control\nhad to come out that way. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:markov-stabilisation-needs-a-fresh-generator","label":"prop:markov-stabilisation-needs-a-fresh-generator","md":"quantum-observable-universe/nuclear-interactions/markov-stabilisation-needs-a-fresh-generator.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.conventionD_markov_defect_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.conventionD_markov_defect_neg","kind":"theorem","line":760,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L760-L771","doc":"**The convention-D Markov parameter is never a root of `x² - h·x - 1`.**\nThe defect is strictly negative for every `q > 0`, so the equation\n`markov_repeat_forces_quadratic` extracts can never be satisfied. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.conventionD_markov_defect","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.conventionD_markov_defect","kind":"theorem","line":745,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L745-L758","doc":"The convention-D defect `z² - h·z - 1` in closed form, with\n`z = q³/(q²+1)` and `h = q - q⁻¹`.\n\nBoth quadratic factors are positive-definite over any linear ordered field\n(`q² ± q + 1 = (q ± 1/2)² + 3/4`), so the defect is a *strictly negative* rational\nfunction of `q` — it has no zero to be found by tuning the pin. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.markov_repeat_forces_quadratic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.markov_repeat_forces_quadratic","kind":"theorem","line":711,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L711-L737","doc":"**Markov stabilisation on a REPEATED generator forces `z² = h·z + 1`.**\n\n`interface_scalar` above takes `tr (W * σ) = z * tr W` as a hypothesis — its own\ndocstring flags this as an axiom standing in for Jones (1987), not a theorem. The\nquestion this answers is *when that hypothesis may be assumed*, which the corpus\nhad not pinned down.\n\nTake the word `W = σ` itself, i.e. the interface generator already occurring once\nin the word it is being appended to. The Hecke quadratic then evaluates the\nleft-hand side outright, and stabilisation becomes an equation on `z` alone.\n\nSo stabilisation is not free: on a repeated generator it *forces* the Markov\nparameter to be a root of `x² - h·x - 1`. Whether that root condition holds is a\nproperty of the convention, settled for convention D below. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.multiset_keyed_energy_well_defined","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.multiset_keyed_energy_well_defined","kind":"theorem","line":683,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L683-L703","doc":"**The ordering-dependence of the nuclear chain is NOT a trace defect.**\n\nBean `qou-c6gx` recorded that the dashboard's binding energy depends on the\nhard-coded nucleon ordering (³He: `ppn` 6.7969 MeV vs `pnp` 7.7794 MeV) and read\nit as a possible violation of trace invariance. It is not one. `ppn` carries the\nbond multiset `{pp, pn}`; `pnp` carries `{pn, pn}`. Different multisets are\ngenuinely different links — *not* conjugates of one another — so different\ntraces are the correct answer.\n\nFormally: an energy keyed on the bond multiset is automatically well defined on\nany two orderings that induce the *same* multiset. When they induce different\nones, the values may legitimately differ, and the ill-posedness lies in the\n*input* — a nucleus has no ordering — not in the trace.\n\nThat is evidence against the chain as a nuclear model (`qou-h7mh`), which is a\nstrictly different complaint from the one `qou-c6gx` filed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.conjugation_reaches_no_new_values","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.conjugation_reaches_no_new_values","kind":"theorem","line":673,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L673-L681","doc":"**…so conjugation enlarges no reachable set.** Packaging of the above for\nthe reachability question: if `S` is the set of interface values attainable by\nmonotone words and `tr b ∈ S`, then `tr (u b u⁻¹) ∈ S` as well. No `S` can grow\nby admitting conjugates. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.trace_conj_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.trace_conj_eq","kind":"theorem","line":647,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L647-L671","doc":"**A conjugated interface has the same trace value.**\n\nThe attack that dies here: `chi_monotone_transfer.word_to_mpfr` rejects\nnon-monotone braid words, so a bond joining strands `1 ↔ 3` must be written\n`σ₁σ₂σ₁⁻¹` and is currently unreachable. The proposed remedy was to lift that\nrestriction and thereby enlarge the set of attainable interface values.\n\nIt attains nothing new. Cyclicity of the Markov trace, `tr (ab) = tr (ba)`,\ngives conjugation invariance for free:\n\n    tr (u b u⁻¹) = tr (u⁻¹ u b) = tr b.\n\nEvery conjugate of `σ₂` carries `σ₂`'s value exactly. Lifting the monotone-word\nrestriction changes which *words* are admissible but not which *numbers* are\nreachable, so the colour-weight blocker — that `span{1, σ₂}` is a 2-plane\ninside the 4-dimensional `V_(2,1)` block — survives untouched. The refutation\nis by derivation, not by measurement.\n\nStated for a bare `u : Aˣ` and an arbitrary codomain `K`: no order, no\ntopology, no algebraic structure on the target is used. Cyclicity alone is the\nwhole hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.common_factor_preserves_degeneracy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.common_factor_preserves_degeneracy","kind":"theorem","line":635,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L635-L639","doc":"The bare form: a factor common to both channels cannot break a degeneracy\nthat already holds. Stated separately because it is what makes the theorem above\nindependent of *why* the space factor is common. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.separable_interface_degenerate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.separable_interface_degenerate","kind":"theorem","line":615,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L615-L633","doc":"**A separable interface stays degenerate on the two pn channels, whatever\nspace index it carries.**\n\nThe hypotheses encode the Pauli lock exactly as `qou-kj4v` states it: the\ntriplet is `(S = 1, T = 0)` and the singlet `(S = 0, T = 1)`, and the *same* two\nprojector scalars `μ₊`, `μ₋` appear in each SU(2) with the roles exchanged. The\nspace factor is applied at one and the same `ell`, which is the physical input —\nboth channels are S-wave.\n\nThe conclusion holds for **every** `fSpace`, so no choice of space index rescues\na separable interface. What the tensor force does in the Standard Model is\n*couple* spin to space; a product of a spin function and a space function is\nprecisely what a coupling is not. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.content_keyed_interface_refuted","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.content_keyed_interface_refuted","kind":"theorem","line":567,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L567-L588","doc":"**Any interface keyed on nucleon CONTENT is refuted by the two pn channels.**\n\nThe proposal \"identical nucleons take two crossings\" — and every variant of it —\nassigns the interface from the unordered pair of nucleon species, i.e. it\nfactors through `content : Channel → Content`. But nature's two pn channels\nhave the *same* content and *opposite* boundness: pn(³S₁) is the deuteron at\n`+2.224566 MeV`, pn(¹S₀) is unbound (a virtual state at `−0.066 MeV`). A\nfunction of the content alone must give them the same energy.\n\nSo the refutation is logical, not numerical: no choice of `m_pp`, `m_nn`,\n`m_pn` can succeed, because the failure is in what the multiplicities are\nallowed to depend on. `mu_singlet_lt_mu_triplet` supplies the variable that\ndoes work — the isospin label `T`, which separates the two pn channels. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:content-keyed-interface-refuted","label":"prop:content-keyed-interface-refuted","md":"quantum-observable-universe/nuclear-interactions/content-keyed-interface-refuted.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.singlet_binds_more","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.singlet_binds_more","kind":"theorem","line":555,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L555-L561","doc":"**…and therefore the singlet binds harder.** `−log` is decreasing, so\n`μ₋ < μ₊` transports to `B₊ < B₋` for any positive scale `α`. Stated over `ℝ`\nbecause `Real.log` is. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prf:isospin-singlet-binds-more","label":"prf:isospin-singlet-binds-more","md":"quantum-observable-universe/nuclear-interactions/isospin-singlet-binds-more-proof.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mu_singlet_lt_mu_triplet","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mu_singlet_lt_mu_triplet","kind":"theorem","line":528,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L528-L553","doc":"**THE ORDERING IS FORCED — no fit, no multiplicity, every `q`.**\n\n`μ₊` is the `T = 1` (isospin-symmetric) interface scalar and `μ₋` the `T = 0`\n(antisymmetric) one. Under convention D,\n\n    μ₋ < μ₊   for every  q > 0,\n\nbecause the difference is `μ₊ − μ₋ = (2z − h)/(q + q⁻¹)` and\n`h_lt_two_z_conventionD` makes the numerator positive. With `B = −α·log μ`\ndecreasing in `μ`, the **isospin singlet is always the more strongly bound\nchannel**. Nature agrees: of the four two-nucleon channels — pp, nn, pn(¹S₀)\nall `T = 1`, pn(³S₁) the deuteron at `T = 0` — exactly the `T = 0` one binds.\n\nThis is the replacement for the crossing-multiplicity story. The channel is\nselected by a representation label, not by a count of crossings, so there is\nno `m_pp` / `m_nn` to tune (cf. bean `qou-h7mh`: \"DO NOT JUST FIT m_*\"). ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:isospin-singlet-binds-more","label":"prop:isospin-singlet-binds-more","md":"quantum-observable-universe/nuclear-interactions/isospin-singlet-binds-more.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mu_sum_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mu_sum_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":517,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L517-L526","doc":"**The two isospin channels partition the free trace.** The scalar shadow of\n`proj_add_eq_one`: `μ₊ + μ₋ = 1` exactly. Projecting onto a channel can only\n*remove* trace weight, so `μ_T < 1` and `B_T = −α·log μ_T > 0` for both `T` —\nwhich is why the projector decomposition alone can never produce an unbound\nchannel. Unbinding needs `μ > 1`, and that requires leaving the convex\ndecomposition of `1`, e.g. `stacked_crossing_gt_one`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.h_lt_two_z_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.h_lt_two_z_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":507,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L507-L514","doc":"**…hence `h < 2z` for every `q > 0`.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.two_z_sub_h_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.two_z_sub_h_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":494,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L494-L505","doc":"**Convention D makes `2z − h` a manifest positive.** With the\nTemperley–Lieb / SU(2) Markov parameter `z = q³/(q²+1)` and the Hecke\nparameter `h = q − q⁻¹`,\n\n    2z − h = (q⁴ + 1) / (q(q² + 1)),\n\nan identity, not an estimate. Every factor on the right is positive for\n`q > 0`, so the sign needs no numerical input. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.proj_mul_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.proj_mul_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":471,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L471-L485","doc":"**…and orthogonal.** `P₊ · P₋ = 0` follows from the quadratic together with\n`h = q − q⁻¹`, which is exactly the Hecke parametrisation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.proj_add_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.proj_add_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":460,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L460-L469","doc":"**The isospin projectors are complementary.** Writing `s = (q + q⁻¹)⁻¹`,\n`P₊ = s•(σ + q⁻¹•1)` and `P₋ = s•(q•1 − σ)` sum to `1`. For two nucleons `T=1`\nis the symmetric channel and `T=0` the antisymmetric one, so the eightfold-way\ndecomposition is already present in the Hecke quadratic — no new parameter. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.tilt_log_shift","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.tilt_log_shift","kind":"theorem","line":434,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L434-L452","doc":"**…so the binding it induces depends only on the ratio `t/μ₀`.**\n\nWith `μ₀` the colour-blind scalar and `t = h·δ/4` the shift, the change in\n`B = −α·log μ` is `−α·log(1 + t/μ₀)` exactly. That is what makes the two roles\nof δ separable: at q₀, `t = 7.19e-5` and `μ₀ = 0.394790`, so `t/μ₀ = 1.82e-4`\nand the entire tilt is worth `7.6e-4 MeV` on `B(²H)` — while the\nleading-vs-subleading disagreement inside δ is a further 1e-3 of *that*.\n\nRole (a) (δ as the physical `Δm/M_N`, gated at 1000 ppm against CODATA) and\nrole (b) (δ as this tilt) therefore cannot disturb one another, and can be\ngiven separate names and separate derivations. Bean `qou-nbx7`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.tilt_is_additive_shift","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.tilt_is_additive_shift","kind":"theorem","line":426,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L426-L432","doc":"**The flavour tilt is an additive shift on μ, of size `h·δ/4`.**\n\nThe p–n template is `d = −h(½ − δ/4)`, which is exactly the colour-blind\n`−h/2` displaced by `h·δ/4`. So the tilt's whole effect on the interface scalar\nis that shift — there is no other channel through which δ enters the trace. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.binding_indep_of_free_trace","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.binding_indep_of_free_trace","kind":"theorem","line":410,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L410-L420","doc":"**The nucleon identity cancels out of the binding.** Since\n`tr_atom = tr_free · μ`, the binding `−α·log tr_atom − (−α·log tr_free)` is\n`−α·log μ`: `tr_free`, and with it `tr_M(p)` and `tr_M(n)`, drops out entirely.\n\nThis is why *all* isospin physics must live in the interface — nothing else\nsurvives the difference. In particular any assignment with `d_pp = d_nn` gives\nidentically zero mirror splitting no matter how the proton and neutron words\ndiffer, which is what `mirror_split_eq_L_diff` sees from the other side. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mirror_split_eq_L_diff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mirror_split_eq_L_diff","kind":"theorem","line":398,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L398-L408","doc":"**The mirror splitting sees only `L_nn − L_pp`; the p–n bond cancels.**\n\nOn the chain topology ³H = `pnn` carries bonds `{pn, nn}` and ³He = `ppn`\ncarries `{pp, pn}`, so the predicted splitting is `α(L_nn − L_pp)` regardless of\nwhat the p–n bond is. Combined with `charge_independence_iff` (`L_pp = L_nn ⟺\nh = 0`) this says the model's mirror splitting is nonzero **precisely because**\nthe σ/σ⁻¹ templates are charge-assigned — it is an artifact, not isospin\nphysics. Measured: `α(L_nn − L_pp) = 2.27077 MeV` against nature's 0.7638, which\nis mostly Coulomb. Giving all three bonds the same template sends it to zero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.weight_eq_proj_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.weight_eq_proj_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":383,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L383-L392","doc":"**…and inverting it reproduces the isospin projector scalar.** Solving\n`markov_param_determined` for `y₂` gives exactly `μ₊ = (z + q⁻¹)/(q + q⁻¹)`, the\nsymmetric-channel interface scalar from `proj_add_eq_one`. So the eightfold-way\nprojector weights and the `n = 2` Wedderburn weights are the *same object*, not\ntwo constructions that happen to agree numerically (they match to 3.6e-31). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.markov_param_determined","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.markov_param_determined","kind":"theorem","line":361,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L361-L381","doc":"**The Markov parameter is not a free choice — the weights fix it.**\n\nAt `n = 2` the two irreps are `(2)` and `(1,1)`, both one-dimensional, with\n`χ^{(2)}(σ) = q` and `χ^{(1,1)}(σ) = −q⁻¹`. Given only the Markov normalisation\n`Σ_λ y_λ d_λ = 1` (here `y₂ + y₁₁ = 1`) and the pairing\n`tr_M(σ) = Σ_λ y_λ χ^λ(σ)`, the parameter `z := tr_M(σ)` is a *function of the\nweights*:\n\n    z = y₂·(q + q⁻¹) − q⁻¹.\n\n`y_lambda_at(n, q)` takes only `q` — there is no `z` argument — so `z` is a\nconsequence of the Jones–Markov hook-length weights, not an input beside them.\nChecked numerically at q₀: `tr_M(σ)` from `y_λ` equals `1/(√q + 1/√q)` to\n7.3e-31 (the 30-digit `y_λ` cap). Consequently z **cannot be retuned** to close\n²H without contradicting `y_λ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.linearForm_ceiling","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.linearForm_ceiling","kind":"theorem","line":343,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L343-L353","doc":"**The linear form's ceiling.** Because the atom trace is positive,\n`B_lin = a(t_free - t_atom)` is strictly bounded by `a * t_free` — no choice of\nword or convention escapes it.\n\n§10a's table is this bound in the open: with `t_free ~ 1` at every `A`, the\nceiling sits near `a ~ 4.19 MeV`, so `B_AME` at ³H (8.48), ⁴He (28.30) and ⁷Li\n(39.24) is unreachable. That is a second, independent reason to reject the linear\nform, and unlike extensivity it does not depend on the convention. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.linearForm_not_extensive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.linearForm_not_extensive","kind":"theorem","line":333,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L333-L341","doc":"**The linear form is not extensive**, whenever neither bond scalar is\ntrivial. `x = 1` or `y = 1` is the degenerate case of a bond that contributes\nnothing, so on any bond that binds at all the linear form fails. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.linearForm_extensivity_defect","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.linearForm_extensivity_defect","kind":"theorem","line":315,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L315-L331","doc":"**The linear form's extensivity defect, in closed form.** For the rival\ncandidate `B = a(1 - t)` the failure is exactly `-a(1-x)(1-y)` — an identity, so\nit needs no positivity hypothesis and no anchor.\n\nThis sharpens §10a, which reports the defect as a single measured number. That\nnumber is **convention-C frame**: the audit is dated one day before the\n2026-08-09 convention-D excision. Re-measured at the proton pin with\n`mu_pp = z`, `mu_nn = z - h`:\n\n    convention C   z = sqrt(q)/(q+1)   defect = 1.487498   <- the audit's 1.4875\n    convention D   z = q^3/(q^2+1)     defect = 0.966887\n\nThe conclusion is unaffected in either convention — the closed form vanishes only\nwhen `x = 1` or `y = 1` — but the quoted figure needs the scope. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:binding-functional-is-the-log","label":"prop:binding-functional-is-the-log","md":"quantum-observable-universe/nuclear-interactions/binding-functional-is-the-log.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.logForm_extensive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.logForm_extensive","kind":"theorem","line":308,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L308-L313","doc":"**The log form is extensive.** A product of bond scalars becomes a sum of\nbond energies — the property §10a requires of the trace-to-energy map. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.alpha_upper_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.alpha_upper_bound","kind":"theorem","line":276,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L276-L280","doc":"The contrapositive as a bound on the energy scale: if the observed bond\nenergy is below `α·log 2`, then `α` is too large to be the interface scale. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.bond_energy_floor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.bond_energy_floor","kind":"theorem","line":252,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L252-L274","doc":"**The universal binding floor.** Combining `mu_le_half` with the fact that\n`-log` is decreasing: for any admissible interface scalar,\n\n    B_bond = -α·log μ  ≥  α·log 2.\n\nNumerically `α·log 2 = 2.90150117984 MeV` while `B_AME(²H) = 2.224566`, which is\n**30.43% below the floor**. Unlike `mu_unreachable`, which was stated at the\nactual `z`, this holds for *every* `q`, every crossing convention, every\nmultiplicity, and every `N` in an `SU(N)` choice of `z` — so it is a statement\nabout **α**, not about the interface.\n\nContrapositive, and the reason bean `qou-qlma` exists: the deuteron is reachable\nat all only if `α ≤ B_AME(²H)/log 2 = 3.2093703 MeV`. The derived\n`α = m_e/(q₀(q₀−1)) = 4.1859814` exceeds that by 30.43%.\n\nStated over `ℝ` rather than a generic ordered field because `Real.log` is\ngenuinely required here — this is not a gratuitous `ℝ` in the sense of §7c. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mu_le_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mu_le_half","kind":"theorem","line":243,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L243-L246","doc":"Every admissible interface scalar is at most `1/2`: `d ≤ 0` from the Hecke\nrange, `z ≤ 1/2` from AM-GM. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.markov_param_le_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.markov_param_le_half","kind":"theorem","line":233,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L233-L241","doc":"**`z ≤ 1/2` for every q.** The production Markov parameter is\n`z_m = 1/(q^{1/2} + q^{-1/2})`; with `x = √q₀` that is `(x + x⁻¹)⁻¹`, bounded by\n`1/2` unconditionally. Wenzl positivity (`q^{1/2} + q^{-1/2} > 2`, already\nrequired by the corpus) makes it strict. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.two_le_add_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.two_le_add_inv","kind":"theorem","line":226,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L226-L231","doc":"**AM-GM on the Markov parameter.** `2 ≤ x + x⁻¹` for every `x > 0`, since\n`x + x⁻¹ - 2 = (x-1)²/x ≥ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.stacked_crossing_gt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.stacked_crossing_gt_one","kind":"theorem","line":219,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L219-L224","doc":"Stacking crossings does not escape the interval either: `σ²  = h•σ + 1`\nsends `(a, b) = (1, 0)` to `(h, 1)`, whose scalar `h·z + 1` exceeds `1` whenever\n`h` and `z` are positive — i.e. the two-crossing bond is *anti*-bound\n(`μ > 1 ⟹ L < 0`). Measured: `μ = 1.1044`, `B = −0.416 MeV`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mu_unreachable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mu_unreachable","kind":"theorem","line":206,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L206-L217","doc":"**The floor theorem.** A target strictly above `z` is attained by no\nadmissible interface element. With `z = 1/(√q₀ + 1/√q₀)` this says\n`B ≥ α·ln(1/z) = 2.907195 MeV` for every crossing convention, while\n`B_AME(²H) = 2.2245662`. **Retracted as a physical claim 2026-08-09**: those are\nconvention-C numbers, and under production convention D the deuteron IS\nreachable at `d = −0.11945·h`. The theorem below is a true conditional and is\nunaffected; only its application to ²H was wrong. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mu_mem_Icc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.mu_mem_Icc","kind":"theorem","line":200,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L200-L204","doc":"The admissible interface offsets `d ∈ [−h, 0]` — σ⁻¹ at one end, σ at the\nother — put `μ = z + d` in `[z − h, z]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.charge_independence_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.charge_independence_iff","kind":"theorem","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L189-L198","doc":"**Charge independence forces the classical limit.** The framework assigns\n`d_pp = 0` (σ) and `d_nn = −h` (σ⁻¹), so the p-p and n-n interface scalars agree\nonly when `h = 0`, i.e. `q = 1`. Since the strong force *is* charge independent,\nthe σ/σ⁻¹ assignment cannot be right at `q ≠ 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.interface_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.interface_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L167-L181","doc":"**The interface collapses to one scalar.** Markov stabilisation\n`tr(W·σ) = z·tr(W)` plus linearity of the trace send every interface element\n`a•σ + b•1` to the single number `μ = a·z + b`.\n\nThe Markov property is a hypothesis, not a theorem: in this corpus it is an\naxiom standing in for Jones (1987). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.interface_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InterfaceScalar.interface_mul","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InterfaceScalar.lean#L151-L165","doc":"**The interface subalgebra is two-dimensional.** The Hecke quadratic\n`σ² = h•σ + 1` closes `span{1, σ}` under multiplication, so a word of *any*\nlength at the single inter-nucleon generator is again `a•σ + b•1`. This is what\nforbids \"adding more knot operations\" at the interface: there is nowhere for\nthem to go. ","signatureChunk":0}]}